+# Audio contract fixtures
+
+These fixtures are deterministic synthetic two-tone audio, generated locally for the YSM audio parser and streaming tests. They contain no user recordings or model-pack audio.
+
+`manifest.json` records the codec, channel count, normalized sample rate, decoded frame count, Opus pre-skip, and short-cache boundary for each encoded file. Vorbis `.s16le` references are mono signed 16-bit little-endian PCM decoded by FFmpeg. Opus references are produced by the Windows x64 native YSM decoder and are the fixed cross-platform baseline; the macOS ARM64 tests compare against those bytes with the existing two-LSB tolerance.
+
+To regenerate them, install FFmpeg with the `libopus` and `libvorbis` encoders, then run:
+
+```powershell
+python tools/generate_audio_test_fixtures.py --ffmpeg C:\path\to\ffmpeg.exe
+$env:YSM_NATIVE_PATH = (Resolve-Path .\src\main\resources\META-INF\native\ysm.dll).Path
+.\gradlew.bat generateOpusFixtureReferences
+```
+
+The generator verifies Ogg page structure, exact granule/frame counts, sample-rate metadata, Opus pre-skip, and PCM reference lengths before writing `manifest.json`. The Opus input-rate case changes only the informational source-rate field in `OpusHead` and recalculates the Ogg page checksum. FFmpeg is needed only to recreate the compressed files and Vorbis references; it is not needed to run the tests.
